  "analysis": "Both responses propose sharding to avoid the single-writer bottleneck and include lanes, gates, security sign‑off, flaky‑check handling, and rollback procedures. PFJ correctly notes the hard single‑writer limit and avoids requiring new storage volumes, instead using local temporary storage and a final merge step, and it adds explicit per‑module timeouts to guard the compute budget. LXZ suggests provisioning three separate cache volumes, which may be infeasible or costly given the hard infrastructure limit and budget concerns, and it lacks the same level of timeout safeguards.",
